<title>USA: USDA scrutinizing EU grain sales, nominee says.</title>
<headline>USDA scrutinizing EU grain sales, nominee says.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>The United States will keep an eye on European Union grain tenders and may retaliate on any unfairly subsidized sales, Gus Schumacher, nominee for Agriculture Department undersecretary, said Wednesday.</p>
<p>During a Senate Agriculture Committee hearing, Schumacher argued for large funding for the major U.S. farm export subsidy program.</p>
<p>&quot;We need to have that tool in reserve if Europeans start to grab market share in markets we need like Egypt,&quot; he said.  </p>
<p>The EU subsidized a grain sale last week but at nominal rates.</p>
<p>Schumacher, now head of USDA's Foreign Agriculture Service, said he would fight aggressively for U.S. exports if confirmed by the Senate.</p>
<p>He cited &quot;tough battles&quot; in the past three years and said more effort might be needed on processed wheat products in the future.</p>
<p>In discussing EU grain export subsidies, Schumacher said European tenders &quot;in the next few weeks will be very telling.&quot;</p>
<p>Schumacher hailed a large U.S. wheat sale to Egypt on Tuesday and said attention might shift to Pakistan.</p>
</text>
